examples.question_text:
A non-square rectangle has a vertical midline and diagonal $AC$ drawn.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75,every node/.style={font=\normalsize,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (4.8,2.6);
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(4.8,2.6);
\draw[black,dashed] (2.4,-0.3)--(2.4,2.9);
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$A$}; \node[above right] at (4.8,2.6) {$C$};
\node[above] at (2.4,2.9) {midline};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Which drawn line is a line of symmetry? Explain using a fold.
(b) State the total number of lines of symmetry.

Which drawn segments are lines of symmetry of this non-square rhombus: $AC$, $BD$, $EF$ or $GH$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (-2.4,0); \coordinate (B) at (0,1.3); \coordinate (C) at (2.4,0); \coordinate (D) at (0,-1.3);
\coordinate (E) at (-1.2,0.65); \coordinate (F) at (1.2,-0.65); \coordinate (G) at (1.2,0.65); \coordinate (H) at (-1.2,-0.65);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (A)--(C) (B)--(D) (E)--(F) (G)--(H);
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[above] {$B$} (C) node[right] {$C$} (D) node[below] {$D$};
\path (E) node[above left] {$E$} (F) node[below right] {$F$} (G) node[above right] {$G$} (H) node[below left] {$H$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Folding along $AC$ makes the top and bottom halves match. Folding along $BD$ makes the left and right halves match.

Folding along $EF$ or $GH$ does not make the halves match. The lines of symmetry are $AC$ and $BD$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (-2.4,0); \coordinate (B) at (0,1.3); \coordinate (C) at (2.4,0); \coordinate (D) at (0,-1.3);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (A)--(C) (B)--(D);
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[above] {$B$} (C) node[right] {$C$} (D) node[below] {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

One possible drawing is:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(4.6,0)--(3.4,1.8)--(0.4,1.8)--cycle;
\draw[->] (2.1,0)--(2.4,0);
\draw[->] (1.7,1.8)--(2,1.8);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The arrow marks show the parallel sides. The sloping sides are not parallel, so there is exactly one pair of parallel sides. The top is off-centre and the sloping sides have different lengths; no fold makes the halves match.
